"P446976328" "Mission Specifics"
// Answer Text :
// Answer Text :
"P2853608941" "Frequently Asked Questions"
// Page BasicFeatures1 Text :
"P231648136" ""<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Basic Walkthrough</i></color><br><br>Stories in Architect are broken up into three logical parts.<br><br><color deepskyblue>Part 1: Story Settings</color><br>Stories are a series of chapters or <i>missions</i> that follow one after the next.<color deepskyblue>Story Title & Description</color><br>The first step is to come up with a name for your story and a short description. When you publish your mission, this information will be what other players see when browsing for stories.<br><br><color deepskyblue>Story Contact</color><br>Next you need to pick a story contact who will tell the story to other players. You need a name for the contact and you need to determine what <i>type</i> of contact they are. You're options are <i>Default, Standard Contacts, Enemy Groups, Objects and Custom</i>.<br>- Default: Is the default hologram contact.<br>- Standard Contacts: Are any and all of the contacts that are already in the game.<br>- Enemy Groups: This allows you to select every enemy we have in our database from Paragon City and the Rogue Isles.<br>Objects: Inanimate objects that can be used as story contacts.<br>- Custom: A story contact that you create yourself, using our custom character creator.<br><br>If you're not sure what to put for the contact, don't worry about it. You can always come back to it or even leave it blank.<br><br>Now, let's look at the next part of creating a story in Mission Architect, the <color orange>The Mission Settings</color>"
// Answer Text :
"P4240094066" "Look at Mission Settings"
// Answer Text :
// Page BasicFeatures2 Text :
"P898282498" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Basic Walkthrough</i></color><br><br><color deepskyblue>Part 2: Mission Settings</color><br>Now that you've named your Story and picked your Story Contact you can begin working on your first mission. In Mission Settings you define all the high level aspects of your mission. What map does it takes place on and who are enemies that populate it? After you've done that, you're ready to move on to part three, <i>Mission Goals</i>."
// Answer Text :
// Page BasicFeatures3 Text :
"P2029192889" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Basic Walkthrough</i></color><br><br>Stories in Architect are broken up into three logical parts.<br><br><color deepskyblue>Part 1: StoryArcs Settings</color><br>StoryArcs are a series of missions put together. The first step is to come up with a name for your StoryArc, a short description and to pick a story contact who will give the arc to other players. If you're not sure what to put, don't worry about it. You can always come back to it or even leave it blank.<br><br><color deepskyblue>Part 2: Mission Settings</color><br>Now that you've named your StoryArc and picked your Story Contact you can begin working on your first mission. In Mission Settings you define all the important aspects of your mission like what map it takes place on and the enemies that populate it. After you've done that, you're ready to move on to the specific mission details.<br><br><color deepskyblue>Part 3: Mission Details</color><br>Here you pick the specifics of what you want in your mission. Do you want a boss the players have to fight or a hostage they have to rescue? Are you writing this for villains so they have to break into an office building and collect some important files off a computer or kidnap the mayor? All of that you define in the Mission Details section.<br><br>Once you've set all of that up, you're ready to test your mission. We recommend you test your mission a couple times, making any tweaks to it to add more life and enjoyment to it. Once you think it's fun, publish it up to our arc server for others to enjoy. When it's published others will be able to play your arc, rate it and give you feedback.<br><br>So, let's get started. Do you have any other questions?"
// Page CreatingArcs Text :
"P2101979025" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Creating Arcs</i></color><br><br>You can create arcs from the Mission Architect Computers located in the main area or in the architect lounge.<br><br><color deepskyblue><i>Testing Arcs</i></color><br>While working on your arc, you can test it as if you were actually playing the missions. While testing you don't gain any experience or earn any tickets. You can also test with a team of other players. Testing arcs is useful to make sure the missions act the way you want them to."
// Page Terminology Text :
"P2776362487" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Terminology</i></color><br><br>Here's some useful terms for you to understand while interacting with the Mission Architect:<br><br><color deepskyblue>StoryArc</color>: A group of missions played in sequential order. Also called, 'arcs'.<br><br><color deepskyblue>Souvenir Clue</color>: A large amount of text unlocked for the player when they complete a StoryArc. This clue can be found in the Clue window under the Architect clue tab.<br><br><color deepskyblue>Contact</color>: The person who gives the player the mission."
// Page PlayingArcs Text :
"P3919248180" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Playing Arcs</i></color><br><br>You can play arcs created by both heroes and villains across all shards. So, if you're playing a hero on Champion you can play an arc written by a villain on Virtue. While playing arcs you'll gain a portion of the experience you would in the 'real world'. You will also gain tickets that you can later use at the store inside Mission Architect."
// Page RatingArcs Text :
"P3639890568" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Rating Arcs</i></color><br><br>You can rate arcs that you play. You can only do this once.<br><br><color deepskyblue><i>Hall of Fame</i></color><br>Arcs that become very popular among the community will earn the 'Hall of Fame' title. Hall of Fame arcs get permanently stored on the <color deepskyblue>Arc Server</color> and free up one of the three arc slots for the person who created it.<br><br><color deepskyblue><i>Dev Choice</i></color><br>Dev Choice arcs are StoryArcs that the developers (us) hand pick as the best of the best. Dev Choice arcs get permanently stored on the Arc Server and free up one of the three arc slots for the person who created it.<br><br><color deepskyblue><i>Flag for Content</i></color><br>Arcs that have inappropriate content can be flagged for customer service to investigate. If an arc gets flagged repeatedly for inappropriate content there is a chance the arc will get pulled from the arc server before customer service investigates it. Having an arc pulled the first time means that the architect will have the option to resolve any issues with the arc or submit the arc directly to customer service for approval. However, if an arc is resubmitted and continues to get flagged for inappropriate content, the arc will get pulled and only customer service will be able to resolve the problem."
// Page PublishingArcs Text :
"P914321713" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Publishing Arcs</i></color><br><br>You can publish arcs and share them with others. You can publish three arcs to the Arc Server. However, you can have as many arcs in development that aren't published as you want. When an arc is published others are able to see your arc, play it and rate it. The better rated your arc becomes the more tickets you'll earn."
// Page ArchitectRewards Text :
"P330645900" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Architect Rewards</i></color><br><br>There are a number of different types of rewards in Mission Architect. Of course, you'll earn experience while playing in the Architect, but you'll also earn architect specific badges as well as what we call Architect Tickets.<br><br><color deepskyblue><i>Architect Tickets</i></color><br>Architect Tickets are similar to our Merits System. As you defeat enemies on an architect map or complete objectives you gain tickets. On mission complete these tickets are granted to you. You can then use these tickets to purchase various rewards from the Mission Architect store.<br><br><color deepskyblue><i>Badges</i></color><br>While playing in Mission Architect you can earn Architect specific badges. However, all other badges are not granted while playing in Mission Architect."
// Page CustomCharacters Text :
"P3194666674" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Custom Characters</i></color><br><br>You can build your own characters and place them within your arcs. You can create your own contact, boss, rescue, escort or ally in the map. You can also create full enemy groups.<br><br><color deepskyblue><i>Custom Contacts</i></color><br>Custom Contacts are characters you can use as your mission giver. You can also use them as people needed to be rescued in missions. However, if you want them to be a boss or an ally, you'll need to give them powers.<br><br><color deepskyblue><i>Enemy Groups</i></color><br>You can build your own enemy groups. You can create Minions, Lieutenants and Bosses to place in the enemy groups. You can even use existing enemies and form your own custom groups."
// Page MissionDetailIntro Text :
"P2422359270" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Mission Specifics</i></color><br><br>We've broken our mission details into two sections, Basic and Advanced.<br><br><color deepskyblue>Basic</color>: Boss, Collection, Defeat All and Rescue.<br><br><color deepskyblue>Advanced</color>: Allies, Ambushes, Battles, Defend, Destroy, Escort and Patrols<br><br>Which section would you like to learn about?"
// Answer Text :
"P3529975141" "Basic Details"
// Answer Text :
"P2078488814" "Advanced Details"
// Page BasicDetails Text :
"P2855034304" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Basic Details</i></color><br><br>Here's information for the Basic Details."
// Answer Text :
"P2899416298" "Bosses"
// Answer Text :
"P4289847662" "Collections"
// Answer Text :
"P2209058793" "Defeat Alls"
// Answer Text :
"P338192692" "Rescues"
// Answer Text :
"P2442286205" "I want to look at the Advanced Mission details."
// Page AdvancedDetails Text :
"P3365095518" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Advanced Details</i></color><br><br>Here's information for the Advanced Details."
// Answer Text :
"P3334032241" "Allies"
// Answer Text :
"P2993553378" "Ambushes"
// Answer Text :
"P2114461491" "Battles"
// Answer Text :
"P4101868985" "Defends"
// Answer Text :
"P2489429722" "Destroys"
// Answer Text :
"P541976214" "Escorts"
// Answer Text :
"P3534899364" "Patrols"
// Answer Text :
"P3245653969" "I want to look at Basic Mission specifics"
// Page AllyDetail Text :
"P2900516645" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Allies</i></color><br><br>An Ally Mission Detail is one where somewhere on the map is an NPC who will team up with you and help you fight. The Ally can be surrounded by enemies or they can be by themselves waiting for you to find them."
// Answer Text :
// Page AmbushDetail Text :
"P1697125808" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Ambushes</i></color><br><br>An Ambush Mission detail is triggered after you've completed another mission detail. For example if you interact with a computer you could set it up so an ambush spawns and come after the player. The trigger is the computer. They can't be set as required for mission complete."
// Page BattleDetail Text :
"P1738309048" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Battles</i></color><br><br>A Battle Mission Detail is one where two enemy groups are fighting with one another. You can set who those enemy groups are as well as some dialog they say to each other and to you when you approach them."
// Page BossDetail Text :
"P3399023535" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Bosses</i></color><br><br>The Boss Mission detail allows for players to fight a major threat in the mission. You can place as many bosses in a map as the map allows. Bosses can be required for mission complete or they can be optional.<br><br>You must specify the boss name and the enemy group of the boss, but you can also change the enemies surrounding the boss, the animations the boss does when approached as well as the alignment. There are also multiple lines of dialog that you can write for bosses as well."
// Answer Text :
// Page CollectionDetail Text :
"P2997582248" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Collections</i></color><br><br>A Collection Mission detail is one where you're sent in to find or interact with some physical object in the map. This could be a computer that you hack, a wall safe you crack or a altar where you must perform a ritual. Collection details are broken down into two categories: Wall and Floor. This determines the general location of where these objects will spawn. Collection details can not be failed."
// Page DefeatAllDetail Text :
"P1001609454" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Defeat Alls</i></color><br><br>A Defeat All Mission detail has two states. Defeat all enemies on the map and defeat all enemies in the end room. These are pretty self explanatory. Defeat all enemies on the map means that you have to defeat every enemy that is in this map in order for the mission to be complete. Defeat all enemies in the end room means that you must defeat all the enemies that are in the last room of the map. This last detail is usually used in conjunction with a boss."
// Page DefendDetail Text :
"P3318285883" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Defends</i></color><br><br>A Defend Mission detail is one where you're sent in to a map to defend a physical object from attackers. The attackers will come in waves against you and the detail is considered complete when you've successfully held off all of the waves. This mission detail can be failed by the player so you need to make sure you write failure dialog for them if this happens."
// Page DestroyDetail Text :
"P1849156600" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Destroys</i></color><br><br>A Destroy Mission detail is one where you're sent into a map to destroy a physical object. The object will be guarded by enemies. The detail is considered complete when you've successfully destroyed the object. This detail can't be failed."
// Page EscortDetail Text :
"P428378372" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Escorts</i></color><br><br>An Escort Mission detail is one where you're sent into a map to find a person and lead them out of the map. It's possible to set the person up so they can be defeated along the way to the exit door. If this is the case, and the person is defeated, the mission will fail."
// Page PatrolDetail Text :
"P338760763" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Patrols</i></color><br><br>A Patrol Mission detail is one where there are enemies that wander around the map. These enemies can have dialog they say to help push the story of the map along. Patrols can't be flagged as required for objective complete. They're simply useful to make the mission feel more alive."
// Page RescueDetail Text :
"P1214105047" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor<br><i>Rescues</i></color><br><br>A Rescue Mission detail is one where you are sent in to find a person and free them from their captors. Once freed, the person will run to the nearest exit. A rescue objective can't be failed and the person who is rescued can not be targeted or defeated."
// Page MoreInformation Text :
"P123549780" "<color deepskyblue>Mission Architect Instructor</color><br><br>Is there anything else I can help you with?"
